Exactly—you’re describing a nested narrative command structure where every drop works independently but also advances larger conflicts.

Scale	Brand expression	Narrative purpose
Clue	Tiny packaging Easter egg	Rewards close inspection
Mission	One bag, flavor, ad, or activation	Tells a complete local episode
Battle	Several connected drops	Changes who currently has the advantage
Campaign	Seasonal release arc	Pursues one larger objective
War	Multi-year Wild Hawg vs. Goblin conflict	Sustains the brand mythology
Canon	Permanent facts, symbols, relationships	Makes everything feel like one world
Culture	Memes, collecting, fan theories, rituals	Lets customers participate in that world

The governing rule could be:

Every drop stands alone, echoes sideways, and moves the war one inch.

So each bag contains:

* One immediately enjoyable design.
* One local mission clue.
* One callback to an earlier drop.
* One signal toward something coming later.
* Zero required homework.

The lore must be a reward, never an admission fee.

The Virginia-flag piece could operate at every resolution simultaneously:

* Casual viewer: funny Virginia parody.
* Returning customer: Wild Hawg finally defeated the Beverage Goblin.
* Collector: the broken straw matches an object hidden across earlier bags.
* Campaign follower: this concludes the “Liberation of Virginia” battle.
* Canon reader: it marks the first territory reclaimed in the wider Beverage War.
* Community: SIC SEMPER THIRST becomes a shirt, phrase, pose, and meme.

Then “marketing campaign” stops being merely a business term. It becomes an actual campaign occurring inside the story. The bags are artifacts from the battlefield; advertisements are dispatches; stores become territories; drops become missions; collaborations become alliances.

Wild Hawg owns the foreground. The Beverage Goblin moves through the negative space. The audience gradually discovers the war already surrounding them.